A||||8||Gold-86SE||Techies Question For Ya - V6 TB Coolant Tubing||||1||
Z||000000||Gold-86SE||03-26-2001||11:38 AM||||<BR>I noticed that the V6 Throttle Body Coolant Tubes, in the first picture on this thread <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/009185.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/009185.html</A>  have been removed.<P>Now, my question is <B><I>how</I></B> does one go about this?  What has to be removed, etc. etc.<P>Does anyone have directions for this mod and what are the advantages?<P>------------------<BR>84 SE 4.10 4 Speed (Not So Fair Weather Driver)<BR>85 GT Automatic (Restoration Project)<BR>86 SE 4 Speed (Fair Weather Daily Driver)<BR>87 SC 5 Speed (Not Running, V8 Conversion?)<BR>Please visit my <A HREF="http://www.trifocus.net/~fws/index.html" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Work Shop</A>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Carrolles||03-26-2001||11:50 AM||||Those tubes actually provide heat to the throttle body to prevent icing up the incoming air stream.  By nature of a venturi effect the throttle body chills the incoming air charge.  Coolant water (actually hot water) is circulated through a channel in the throttle body to keep it warm.  I've read that several members have removed the tubes from the throttle body and blanked them off at the other end (near the thermostat housing) with no bad effects to keep the incoming air cool.  I'm not sure what measurable positive effects you might see though.||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||03-26-2001||12:46 PM||||It's one of those things. 99% of the time, blocking the tubes off has no ill effect. Then one very cold day, you stomp the throttle only to discover that the TB iced up and is stuck at WOT.<P>Just keep that in mind. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||FIEROCIOUS||03-26-2001||02:54 PM||||Actualy, the throttlebody will only ice up in the closed or idle position.<BR>The reason being is the air goes through the small crack, this raises air velocity thus iceing the TB. Your engine has less pressure drop at WOT then at idle, remeber engine vacume? So if it is 1% chance at idle it is like .000000000001% at WOT.<BR>Iceing is most likely to happen in a high humidity low temperature situation but will NEVER cause the TB to be stuck in the WOT position. Worse case being; your car will idle at 2000 till the engine warms. Wich the funny thing is the engine has to be warm to heat the coolant to heat the TB.<P><p>[This message has been edited by FIEROCIOUS (edited 03-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierosound||03-26-2001||03:05 PM||||That's my engine setup.  I ran it through the winter without any problems.  We had some temps near -30C here.  We did have the odd day with high humidity (ice fog conditions) and I never had any problems with icing up.||||reg||10
Z||000005||Gold-86SE||03-26-2001||03:22 PM||||<BR>Ok, sounds like it can and has been done, but how do I do it?<P>Is the any real improvement to performance when this is disabled?||||reg||1
Z||000006||WKDFIRO||03-26-2001||03:42 PM||||Aside from purely aesthetic reasons I had the coolant pipes taken out and the ends welded up, (this was when the engine was being rebuilt.)<P>Since Im in Southern California where the coldest wintery day is a heat wave for most of the eastern seaboard I haven't had ANY problems with throttle ice up. Its been a good 4 years now since the rebuild with no negative effects.<P>There are no performance improvements that I have or heard of from doing this. It just looks better visually.||||reg||6
Z||000007||1FST2M6||03-26-2001||04:57 PM||||tubes welded? i just use a piece of 3/8s or 5/16" transmission/fuel/coolant hose and just ditched the hard pipes.. relocated the MAP sensor to the strut mount wall by the dog bone... <P><BR>how to...<P>make sure you do with with the car STONE COLD! <P>ok here we go.. - remove the intake tube and unscrew the 12mm compression fittings on the TB. some coolant will drain out. once both lines have been un screwed from the TB. you'll see a 12point 7mm bolt on the left side of the intake pelenum. remove this shiney bolt and the hose should drop a bit. on the right side of the intake you'll see the MAP sensor.. unplug it and try not to break the hard plastic vacuum lines running to it. once this has been done you'll see a 10mm black screw that goes through the back of the MAP sensor braket into the intake pelenum remove that 10mm screw. ok there is NOTHING holding the tubes now.. remove the passengerside battery cover/grill vent. trace the hard pipes to the soft tubing at the thermostat housing.. remove both pliable hoses at the thermostat housing. the easiest way i've found to pull the hard pipes out is to cut them at the mount on the drivers side... if you just pull you may pull the vacuum line off the fuel pressure regulator. thats BAD! once you figured out the magic of hard pipe removal... take the clamps that were already on the stock soft hoses and cut a piece about 8" long. attach the NEW transmission/fuel/coolant hose to the 2 nipples that the factory tied into and just make a run from one to the other... make sure you tightend the hose clamps.. reinstall the MAP sensor, thats it... put the grill back on the car.. start it up and hose off the coolant fron the Y-pipe. WITH THE CAR RUNNING! and don't use a pressure washer... just running water.. if you do this with the car off and it don't start take the distributor cap off.. spray the inside with WD40 (it displaces the water) and reinstall.. it'll start now.. (old 4x4 trick) ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Gold-86SE||03-27-2001||12:18 AM||||<BR>Thanks for the help guys, it is appreciated.  Though, I still have yet to decide, if I want to do this mod.||||reg||1
